---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../../components/Disclaimer.astro';

// Term first-use explanation is mandatory on any page using "solar generator".
// docs/04-compliance.md section 3.1.
---

<Base
  title="How to size a solar generator"
  description="Watts versus watt-hours, why panel wattage is not the same as charge rate, and how to work out the capacity you need."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-16">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-4xl">How to size a solar generator</h1>

    <p class="mt-6 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      A "solar generator" is a battery with an optional solar input. It does not generate
      electricity, and without panels it is limited to whatever you charge into it. The search term
      is useful because that is what people type, but the device is a battery, and sizing it is
      about two separate numbers that are easy to confuse.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Watts and watt-hours are not the same</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      Watts measure how fast energy flows. Watt-hours measure how much you have stored. A station
      rated at 1,000 W of output can still hold only 500 Wh of energy, which means it can run a
      1,000 W load for about half an hour, not for a whole night.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      When you look at a spec sheet, the output rating tells you what it can start. The capacity
      tells you how long it lasts. You need both to clear your load, and they are checked
      independently.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Panel wattage is not charge rate</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      A 400 W panel does not deliver 400 W into the battery. Panel ratings assume full sun at a
      specific temperature, and real conditions rarely match. Angle, cloud, season, and heat all
      reduce output, and the charge controller converts at a loss.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      A practical planning figure is that you will see somewhere between half and three quarters of
      the panel's nameplate rating on a good day. Treat solar as a way to extend a stay rather than
      a way to run a load indefinitely.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Working out your number</h2>
    <ol class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        List the devices you need and the hours per day for each. Multiply watts by hours to get
        watt-hours.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Apply a duty cycle to anything with a compressor or thermostat. A fridge does not draw its
        nameplate wattage continuously.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Divide by inverter efficiency, roughly 0.85, to account for conversion losses.
      </li>
      <li class="max-w-[68ch]">
        Add a reserve margin. Fifteen percent is tight, twenty is standard, more than thirty if you
        expect cold weather.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-sm text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      The <a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href="/calculator/">calculator</a> does
      all four steps and shows you which of capacity or output is the binding constraint.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What panels will and will not do</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-[68ch] text-ink-600 dark:text-ink-300">
      Panels are best understood as a top-up. If your daily use is 1,500 Wh and your panel array
      realistically delivers 300 W for five good hours, you can recover most of a day's use. If your
      use is a space heater at 1,500 W continuous, no practical array on a portable station will keep
      up, and the honest answer is that this is the wrong device for that job.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-10 max-w-[68ch] text-sm">
      Next:
      <a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href="/guides/watts-vs-watt-hours/">watts versus watt-hours</a>,
      {' '}
      <a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href="/calculator/">size the load in the calculator</a>
      or read
      <a class="text-moss-700 underline dark:text-moss-300" href="/guides/cpap-backup-power/">how to estimate CPAP backup power</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <Disclaimer electrical />
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
